\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{A Gaussian scalar and a Berezin volume}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{The partial coefficient map in \ref{fdip-00IX} is surjective because #{\int\extd\bar\psi_E\,\extd\psi_E\;(F\Omega_E)=F}; for #{E\ne0} it annihilates the embedded retained algebra. A separately normalized Gaussian functional is
##{F\longmapsto(\det_E D)^{-1}\int\extd\bar\psi_E\,\extd\psi_E\;\left(F \ue^{-\bar\psi_E(D\psi_E)}\right),}
which does take #{1} to #{1}.}

\p{For the purely odd space #{\Pi E}, the convention in \citet{v2, Section 9.5, p. 126}{etingof2024mathematical} is #{\operatorname{Ber}(\Pi D)=(\det_E D)^{-1}}. Thus the Gaussian scalar is #{\det_E D=\operatorname{Ber}(\Pi D)^{-1}}. Evaluating that Gaussian and changing a Berezin volume are different operations. The involution #{\Gamma} on the spin carrier is independent of this parity shift; all retained and eliminated field generators are odd.}
